WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.860
Take a long straight wire
carrying a steady current.

2
00:00:03.910 --> 00:00:06.740
Measure the field a
short distance from it.

3
00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:12.050
Now bend the same wire into a circle,
with that distance as its radius.

4
00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:13.110
Same current.

5
00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.520
Same distance.

6
00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:19.330
Yet at the middle of the loop, the
field is about three times stronger.

7
00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.520
Why should bending a wire
make such a difference?

8
00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:26.110
In this mission, you'll split
a current into tiny elements,

9
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.650
and add up the field that each one makes.

10
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:32.410
Then you'll use symmetry to find
the field on the axis of a loop,

11
00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.490
and beside a straight segment of wire.

12
00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:40.050
Your mission: explain why the loop
gives about three times the field.
